# The dev loop — no change is done without a verify

A change is finished only when the gate it owes has passed. "It compiled"
or "the diff looks right" is not a result. Run, then report the gate.

## Gate tier by what you touched

```
docs/, .cursor/rules, comments, tests themselves → npm run verify:quick   (~3 s)
renderer/panel/*, renderer/interact/*, renderer/*.js core (job, ui, fit…) → npm run verify         (~15 s)
generators/*, cnjob.ts, job.js contract, package.json deps                → npm run verify:full    (~4 min)
any merge / upstream port / multi-module refactor                         → npm run verify:full
```

When unsure between two tiers, run the higher one. `verify` never asks
you questions; a red line is a real signal — fix, don't rationalize.

## The architecture is frozen — fit in, don't reshape

The layered structure (shell → registered modes/editors → module dirs →
neutral layers → `gen/*`) is a **fixed contract, not a suggestion**. New
modules and features slot into it:

- New interaction mode → new file in `renderer/interact/` + `S` slots +
  MODE registration in the shell. It does **not** earn a new directory,
  a new dispatch layer, or a rewrite of the shell.
- New editor → new file in `renderer/panel/` + `@tags` header +
  registration in the shell. Same rule.
- New helper → the owning neutral layer (`fit.js`, `boardSketch.js`,
  `widgets.js`/`shared.js` per the dependency rules). Never a new
  "utils" file beside it, never a shell import.
- New generator → `generators/<module>/` following an existing module's
  file layout; bundled under `renderer/gen/` by `build:generators`.

**Never** reorganize directories, rename layers, split or merge shells,
add a state library / router / framework, or change the `S`-slot
contract on your own initiative. If you believe a structural change
would help, **stop and ask the user** — the architecture only changes
when the user explicitly requests it. `check:deps` enforces the
mechanical part; this rule covers the judgement part it can't see.

## Rules that prevent regressions at the source

1. **Smallest diff that says it.** No drive-by refactors, no renames you
   didn't need — every moved line is a merge-conflict debt for upstream.
2. **Owning file only.** Follow `cab-lab-index` routing; add the feature
   to the module that `@owns` the concept, not to wherever the cursor
   happened to be. Shared helpers go to `widgets.js`/`shared.js`/`fit.js`
   by the layer rules, never to a shell.
3. **New file → new tags.** Header line `// @module … @owns …` + run
   `npm run module-map` to regenerate `docs/MODULE-MAP.md` in the same
   commit. `check:map` fails on stale maps.
4. **Generator output changed on purpose?** You must prove intent:
   `node scripts/diff-snapshots.mjs --regen` → read the field-level diff →
   only then `npm run regen:snapshots`. Never regen to make a red suite
   green without reading the diff — that's how silent regressions ship.
5. **New behaviour → new pin.** Bug fixes and new generator behaviour get
   a regression test in the same change (see docs/TESTING.md layers).
   An un-pinned fix will regress.
6. **Cross-repo data shape** (`cnjob`, `job` contract, snapshot fields):
   `verify:full` is the floor — replay must stay green, and OmniCam's
   golden (`CABINETNC_UPDATE_GOLDENS`) only moves with a stated reason.
7. **Picked between architectures?** Append 3 lines to
   `docs/DECISIONS.md` (what / why / rejected option) in the same
   commit — that's the institutional memory the next session reads.

## What to report

State the gate you ran and its result, e.g. "`npm run verify` — 19/19
green". If a step was skipped, say which and why. A change pushed without
a verify line is treated as unfinished work.
